<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Aydinids</h1><p class="st-meta">1308 CE – 1426 CE · capital Birgi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Aegean beylik whose fleets under Umur Bey raided the Greek world and provoked a crusade against Smyrna.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aydinoglu-mehmed-bey">Mehmed Bey</a>, a commander in the service of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/germiyanids">Germiyanids</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birgi">Birgi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ayasoluk</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus</a>) and the upper <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> citadel from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in the first decade of the 14th century and founded his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, with Birgi as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aydinoglu-mehmed-bey">Mehmed</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umur-bey">Umur Bey</a> built a fleet at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> and raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> fought in the Byzantine civil war. In 1344 a crusading league took Smyrna's harbour castle, founding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crusader-smyrna">Crusader Smyrna</a>, and Umur was killed trying to retake it in 1348. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ayasoluk</a> prospered from Italian trade, and Isa Bey built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-bey-mosque">Isa Bey Mosque</a> there in 1375.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in 1390, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored it in 1402, and after the resistance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneyd-bey">Cuneyd Bey</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it finally in 1425-1426.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aydinids.jpg" alt="Aydinids" width="320" height="278"><figcaption>Image: commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aydin_in_14_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1308 CE – 1426 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Birgi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mehmed Bey, Umur Bey, Cuneyd Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aydinid Beylik, Aydınoğulları, Beylik of Aydin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1367&amp;focus=aydinids" aria-label="Aydinids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5378 803 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aydinids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5698 1059L5741 1021M5698 1059L5709 970M5698 1059L6582 1190"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5741 1021h0M5709 970h0M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5698 1059h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5698 1059h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Birgi. <a href="/atlas?year=1367&amp;focus=aydinids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

